 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "Drive over a bridge and you'll feel it: thump, thump. That's a metal gap in the road.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It looks like a mistake. It's actually what keeps the bridge from breaking itself.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Here's the problem. Almost everything grows a little when it gets hot.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A steel or concrete bridge gets longer every summer afternoon.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And shorter again every winter night.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "For a deck one hundred meters long, that's about six centimeters of movement.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now imagine you build it with no gap at all.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "On a hot day, the deck pushes against the ends with enormous force.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It has nowhere to go, so it buckles upward, or cracks its own supports.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Railroads see the same thing: rails can bend sideways in a heat wave.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So engineers leave a gap, and cover it with steel fingers that slide between each other.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "In summer the fingers slide together. In winter they pull apart.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Big bridges also rest on bearings, so the whole deck can slide on its supports.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Your car rolls right over, and the bridge gets to breathe.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So that thump is the sound of a bridge that won't crack. What should we explain next?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
